@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Add input and the mouse") {
        Pick up the task list from ``TerminalAppProtocol`` basics and make
        it a real tool: a prompt that adds tasks, and toggling by mouse
        click or Enter. Along the way you'll meet the rule every TUI with a
        text field runs into — global keys must be gated the moment letters
        belong to an input.

        Start from where the first tutorial ended, or
        [download the finished project](https://dardevelin.github.io/terminal-ui/downloads/build-a-task-list.zip)
        and edit from there.

        @Image(source: "demo-ch2.gif", alt: "Typing a new task into the prompt, then toggling it done.")
    }

    @Section(title: "A prompt for new tasks") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            ``PromptInput`` is the framework's ready-made input line —
            cursor movement, editing, paste, and submit handling included.
            Building one by hand means cursor math and paste handling that
            mostly works; reach for the component wherever your app takes
            free text — a search box, a filter, a command line. It reads
            and writes your state through two ``Binding``s, so the store
            stays the single source of truth.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a `draft` string and a `PromptInputState` to the store,
                an `addDraft()` that turns the draft into a task, and the
                ``PromptInput`` above the footer with `onSubmit` calling it.

                Some keys changed meaning. Letters must reach the field, so
                the `j`/`k` chords are gone, and so is quitting on `q` — a
                task called "quick fix" would end the app at its first
                letter; Escape quits, and Ctrl+C always does. Space types a
                space now, so toggling waits for the next section. The
                arrows still move the selection, but in a ``PromptInput``
                they recall earlier entries, and every key reaches `onEvent`
                first and the focused view after — so the prompt gives them
                up through `.textKeymap`, where an empty list unbinds a key,
                and keeps Ctrl+P and Ctrl+N for history.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks2-01-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks2-01-01.png", alt: "Typing a new task into the prompt at the bottom of the app.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Toggle by click, and by Enter") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Mouse support needs no setup: SGR mouse events arrive already
            parsed, and `.onClick` on any view gives you its clicks with
            coordinates relative to that view. No hit-testing code, no
            screen math — which makes any view you draw clickable, not
            just the framework's buttons.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Give each row an `.onClick` that toggles that task by id —
                the closure captures the row's own task, so there is
                nothing to look up.

                Enter gets a second job, gated on the draft: with text
                typed it submits; with nothing typed it toggles the
                selected task. One key, two meanings, cleanly separated by
                state.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks2-02-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks2-02-01.png", alt: "A task toggled done from the keyboard, checkbox filled.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Finishing touches") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Small affordances tell the user what the app can do before
            they read anything — and in a terminal, with no menus to
            browse, an undocumented key might as well not exist. A badge
            on the prompt and a footer that names every gesture close
            that gap.

            Up next: <doc:GiveItStructure> splits the app into tabs and
            panes once one screen stops being enough.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add the `badge` to the prompt and update the key-hint
                footer. The app now supports keyboard-only use, mouse
                toggling, and free text entry — with no mode switching
                and no conflicts.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks2-03-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks2-03-01.png", alt: "The finished app: a new task added by typing, toggled done, with a badged prompt and full key hints.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            Why did the `j`/`k` selection chords have to go when the prompt arrived?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                A global handler on a typeable key fires while the user is
                writing — pressing `j` mid-word would move the selection.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    Global chords must never claim keys a focused text
                    input needs. Even a non-typing key can collide: the
                    prompt wanted the arrows for history, and had to give
                    them up through its keymap.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                `PromptInput` reserves every letter key for itself.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The prompt only receives what reaches it — `onEvent`
                    sees every key first, which is exactly why the app
                    must be disciplined about what it grabs globally.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Arrow keys are faster than `j`/`k`.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Speed is not the issue — conflict is. Arrows type no
                    character, but the prompt still had a use for them;
                    the app keeps them only because the prompt's keymap
                    gives them up.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Why can each row's `.onClick` closure toggle the right task
            without any coordinate math?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                The framework hit-tests the click and delivers it to the
                view it landed on, with coordinates local to that view —
                and the closure already captured its own row's task.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    The click arrives already belonging to the row that
                    received it; the handler never needs to know where
                    that row sits on screen.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                The closure searches the task list by the click's screen
                position.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Screen coordinates would force every handler to know
                    its own layout position — the framework resolves that
                    for you.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Each row registers its rectangle with the store on every
                render.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    No registry exists — hit-testing is the framework's
                    job, and your view code never tracks its own geometry.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Why does ``PromptInput`` take two bindings — `text` and `state`?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                `text` is the draft your app owns; `state` carries the
                editor's internals (cursor, history) — both live on your
                store so the source of truth stays yours.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    Components read and write through bindings; they never
                    own your data.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                One is for reading and one is for writing.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Every ``Binding`` is two-way; the split is by what the
                    data is, not by direction.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                `state` is required only when history is enabled.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The state binding is part of the initializer — the
                    prompt always persists its editing state through it.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
